[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "ADR-2608196600"
  :adr/title "49 of 73 shadow-source-path findings were conditional on taking the fix they suggested"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-19"
  :adr/tags "detectors shadow-cljs false-positives source-paths co-scientist"
  :adr/body
  "## How this started

Iteration 28 began by reading the live detector state and finding one entry with
a `SCANNED	0`-shaped problem of its own: `verify-source-path-pins` was
`:inconclusive` at 328 ms, `:last-exit 2`, from a run at 19:39 while the shared
checkout was still behind main. Re-run today it is healthy -- `ok exit 1 14s 217
finding(s) scanned=383` -- and the live state has been refreshed. Reading its
output turned up a finding keyed `orgs/orgs/kotoba-lang`, with the segment `orgs`
twice.

## The real defect that key pointed at

`orgs/etzhayyim/com-etzhayyim-app-explorer/shadow-cljs.edn` declared
`\"../../orgs/kotoba-lang/kotodama/src\"`. From that directory `../../` is already
`orgs/`, so the path resolved to `orgs/orgs/kotoba-lang/kotodama/src` -- a
directory that does not exist. **Spelled correctly it still would not have
worked**: `kotodama/src` holds `kotodama/*.cljc` and has no `kotoba/datom.cljc`.
`kotoba.datom` lives in `kotoba-lang/kotoba-datom`, and **five files require it**
(`chain/datom.cljs`, `chain/agent.cljs`, three tests). This build could not
compile.

Fixed in `etzhayyim/com-etzhayyim-app-explorer` (`e899b55c`), west pin advanced
(`ded147787d0`). Measured on a sandbox tree with the same scanner both times:
before, 9 unresolved namespaces including `kotoba.datom`; after, 8, with
`kotoba.datom` gone.

## The trap in the suggestion

The scanner also suggested adding `\"../../kotoba-lang/dom-gpu/src\"` to resolve
`reagent.core`. **Taking that suggestion would have silently replaced this app's
renderer.** `dom-gpu/src/reagent/core.cljc` is a compatibility shim -- *\"Existing
CLJS code can require [reagent.core :as r] and target the virtual DOM substrate
instead of React/browser DOM\"* -- and source-paths win over jars, so the app's
declared `reagent 1.2.0` dependency would have been shadowed by a WASM-backed
namespace of the same name. The fix hint is a **candidate**, not a prescription.

## What the experiment showed

Same repo, same narrow fix, same scanner, one variable -- whether an unrelated
repo exists elsewhere in the tree:

    dom-gpu present in the tree  ->  exit 1, 8 unresolved (3 of them kotoba.wasm.*)
    dom-gpu absent from the tree ->  exit 0, \"all resolve\"

The walk deliberately continues **through** a namespace the workspace can provide
(the code says so: otherwise the tool reports one link per run and the user
rediscovers the cascade by hand). The consequence, unstated until now, is that
the unresolved list includes namespaces that only become required **if you take
the suggestion** -- and those entries have no `:blamed` requirer, which the code
itself calls the tell: *\"an unresolved name with no requirer would mean the walk,
not the build, is wrong.\"*

## Change

Split by that same test, in the prose and in the FINDING lines:

- a namespace some file in the repo requires -> `fail`, unchanged wording
- a namespace nothing in the repo requires  -> `warn`, and the detail says it was
  reached through a candidate and becomes required only if the path is added

Measured across the whole tree: **73 findings, all `fail`, became 24 `fail` and 49
`warn` -- 67% were conditional.** Finding keys are byte-identical, so no NEW or
RESOLVED churn in `manifest/orgs-detectors-state.edn`; only the severity and the
sentence change.

## Known limitation, measured and not fixed

The scanner does not read `:dependencies`, so a namespace provided by a jar is
reported as unresolved whenever some repo in the workspace happens to ship a file
of the same name. `reagent.core` and `re-frame.core` remain `fail` for this app
even though `reagent 1.2.0` and `re-frame 1.4.3` are declared -- they keep their
`:blamed` requirer, so the blame test cannot separate them. Fixing that means
mapping dependency coordinates to namespace prefixes; not attempted here.

## Not claimed

The app was **not compiled**. It has no `node_modules`, `shadow-cljs` is not
installed globally, and a build is a serialized heavyweight operation under the
resource governor. The evidence for the fix is the scanner's before/after on a
sandbox tree plus a complete accounting of all 9 namespaces: 1 fixed by the new
source-path, 5 from declared `:dependencies`, 3 phantoms that this ADR reclassifies."}]
